 ## Abstract
 
-Joint perturbation measurements can refine individual intervention contributions or reveal changes that depend on the partner. WitnessCell distinguishes these gains and learns partner corrections that endpoint refinement cannot supply. Its core predictor ranks first among sixteen entries in a four-dataset benchmark, including double perturbations. A functional procedure fixed before Jurkat fitting reduces complete-response and partner-contrast errors by 6.43% and 6.18% across 617 held-out pairs. Further functional analyses reveal that one correction can improve complete responses while harming partner prediction. Training-based component control attenuates the partner correction while preserving its endpoint contribution, reducing some harmful changes without uniformly shrinking the predictor. RNA readout shrinkage similarly reduces adverse corrections, with benefits concentrated in a transcript subset. Controlled causal analysis establishes how joint measurements resolve target ambiguities. WitnessCell provides tools to learn, evaluate and selectively adjust predictions of unmeasured combinations.
+Joint perturbation measurements can improve prediction by refining individual intervention contributions or learning partner-dependent changes. WitnessCell uses this distinction to learn combination responses and selectively adjust predictions: it preserves endpoint contributions and sets partner-correction strength using measurements from internally held-out combinations. Its core predictor ranks first among sixteen entries in a four-dataset benchmark, including double perturbations. A functional procedure fixed before Jurkat fitting reduces complete-response and partner-contrast errors by 6.43% and 6.18% across 617 held-out pairs. Retrospective functional analyses show how component control can reduce partner damage while retaining useful endpoint contributions. RNA readout shrinkage also reduces adverse corrections, with benefits concentrated in a transcript subset. Controlled causal analysis establishes how joint measurements resolve target ambiguities. WitnessCell connects response learning with targeted adjustment of predictions for unmeasured combinations.
 
 Combinatorial perturbation screens measure how a cell's response to one intervention changes when another is added. Predicting unmeasured combinations would extend these screens and help researchers study gene function across cellular contexts.[1,2,3] A practical starting point is a collection of single-perturbation measurements and a partially measured combination screen. The immediate goal is to predict the remaining expression or functional responses. However, a reduction in overall prediction error leaves an important question open: did the model estimate each intervention's contribution more accurately, or did it learn changes that depend on the partner?
 
