 WitnessCell addresses this distinction within a prediction method. Its core combines single responses nonlinearly and learns a correction from measured combinations. We show that this correction refines contributions associated with individual intervention identities, termed endpoints, while leaving partner contrasts unchanged. We then construct additional corrections that can change those contrasts and test them against the same fitted baseline. A controlled causal system examines the information needed for this task: the choice of joint measurements can determine whether mechanisms giving different answers to an unmeasured combination can be distinguished.[13]
 
-We evaluate the core predictor across four transcriptomic datasets, then test the additional partner learners in functional and RNA measurements. The functional analysis asks whether partner correction improves unmeasured growth responses when experimental support expands and when a fixed procedure is fitted in another cellular environment. The RNA analysis asks whether agreement between training measurements helps control the correction applied to each transcript. We further ask whether the endpoint and partner parts of a learned correction should receive the same adjustment. Training-based control separates those operations, and held-out measurements test their practical effect. The supplied interfaces fit response models, predict missing combinations and return both the original and adjusted corrections.
+WitnessCell further uses this distinction to control learned predictions. It retains the endpoint contribution of an added correction and determines the strength of its partner component from internal held-out combination measurements. We evaluate the core predictor across four transcriptomic datasets, then test the additional partner learners in functional and RNA measurements. The functional analysis asks whether partner correction improves unmeasured growth responses when experimental support expands and when a fixed procedure is fitted in another cellular environment. Comparisons of original, wholly attenuated and component-controlled predictions test the value of retaining endpoint contributions when partner corrections are attenuated. The RNA analysis asks whether agreement between training measurements helps control the correction applied to each transcript. The supplied interfaces fit response models, predict missing combinations and return both the original and adjusted corrections.
 
 ## Results
 
-### WitnessCell separates endpoint refinement from partner-dependent prediction
+### WitnessCell separates response components and controls partner corrections
 
 WitnessCell starts from control and available single-perturbation measurements, observed combinations and the identities of requested combinations. Its transcriptomic core, the configuration used in the benchmark, uses measured single effects when available and estimates missing ones from training responses and gene annotations. It combines the single effects through a saturation rule, then transfers residuals from measured combinations that share endpoints with the query (Fig. 1a). Training or validation measurements determine the fitted settings. All measurements from each test combination are excluded from model fitting. The resulting predictor can use combination data even when some requested single effects have not been measured.
 
